def apply_block(utxos, block):
    new = dict(utxos)
    fees = 0
    for tx in block:
        total_in = 0
        for outpoint in tx["inputs"]:
            if outpoint not in new:
                raise ValueError(f"{tx['txid']}: {outpoint} is missing or already spent")
            total_in += new.pop(outpoint)
        total_out = sum(tx["outputs"])
        if any(v <= 0 for v in tx["outputs"]) or total_out > total_in:
            raise ValueError(f"{tx['txid']}: bad output values")
        for i, value in enumerate(tx["outputs"]):
            new[(tx["txid"], i)] = value
        fees += total_in - total_out
    return new, fees

exercises.solution:
Remove each spent outpoint from a working copy as soon as it is used. A second spend, whether in the same transaction or a later one, then finds it missing; and an output created later in the block is not yet in the copy when an earlier transaction looks for it.

```python
def apply_block(utxos, block):
    new = dict(utxos)
    fees = 0
    for tx in block:
        total_in = 0
        for outpoint in tx["inputs"]:
            if outpoint not in new:
                raise ValueError(f"{tx['txid']}: {outpoint} is missing or already spent")
            total_in += new.pop(outpoint)
        total_out = sum(tx["outputs"])
        if any(v <= 0 for v in tx["outputs"]) or total_out > total_in:
            raise ValueError(f"{tx['txid']}: bad output values")
        for i, value in enumerate(tx["outputs"]):
            new[(tx["txid"], i)] = value
        fees += total_in - total_out
    return new, fees
```

The running time is linear in the number of inputs and outputs, thanks to dictionary lookups (see [hash tables](/en/cs/hash-tables/)). Real nodes keep the UTXO set in a database (Bitcoin Core uses LevelDB with a large in-memory cache) because it holds well over a hundred million outputs, and they check signatures and scripts too, which dominates the cost. A real block would also have a coinbase whose outputs may claim at most the subsidy plus `total_fees`.

